Revert "input-selector: implement a two-phase sinkpad switch" and its
five upstream follow-up fixes, back to the single-mutex active-pad
design GStreamer used before 1.26.11.

Upstream introduced a two-phase active-pad switch protected by a
GRWLock (active_sinkpad_lock) plus a pending_active_sinkpad/
maybe_commit_active_pad() commit step, replacing the previous design
where the whole element state (including the active pad) was
protected by a single GMutex (GST_INPUT_SELECTOR_LOCK). This is meant
to let a buffer/event already in flight on the streaming thread keep
using a stable "current" active pad while a property change
(current-text on playbin, etc.) is pending, only "committing" the
switch at a safe point.

In practice this two-phase RWLock design is deadlock-prone: GStreamer
issue #5150 ("input-selector: Deadlock when releasing an inactive
sinkpad while srcpad receives an upstream event during a buffer push")
documents a real deadlock between a writer-lock request and two
readers already in flight, and the reporter notes their libc's rwlock
writer-preference behaviour affects how reliably it reproduces -
exactly the kind of fairness/ordering variance embedded Linux libc
implementations differ on too, not something specific to their
platform.

On this OpenViX build, selecting a text (subtitle) track on a file
with more than one embedded subtitle track deadlocks any later
flushing seek on playbin's internal text input-selector: the main
thread blocks inside gst_element_seek() (which needs the
active_sinkpad_lock via a property set on current-text), while the
streaming thread pushing a subtitle buffer already holds a reader lock
through gst_selector_pad_chain(). Neither subsink's sync/async
properties nor sync-streams affect this, since the block is in
input-selector's own locking, not anything sink-side.

Reverting to the pre-1.26.11 single-mutex design removes the RWLock
and the pending/commit indirection entirely - the active pad is read
and written directly under the one GST_INPUT_SELECTOR_LOCK mutex,
same as every other piece of this element's state, matching the
"it just works" design GStreamer core carried for years before this
rework.

This reverts, as a set, gstreamer commits:
  dd0c2e3ebadb55341900f88091ccd6f9bebcea30 "input-selector: implement a two-phase sinkpad switch"
  b7cabc85f2cc02d55bdbc822fde4c112e618aa26 "input-selector: fix race condition selecting next active pad"
  192632b3121712c5a0e2cc283265fcdf6e65e210 "input-selector: gracefully handle active pad disappearance"
  2842f2aa1d962b21ee78062e003ab1fed217eba4 "input-selector: don't consider pad being released checking for eos"
  81125657be1500977c4b3d0f9d76c2a965084b11 "input-selector: fix regression receiving eos event"
  215d8a5ac4939d794e76738e394bf4d6f73b9ae3 "input-selector: don't wait for active pad when eos (active-segment mode)"
  06a28619acd994b21c98301e9f411d7d7366d52f "inputselector: Hold active-pad during allocation query"
  0eafdffcf68c386b94629c2e863d837d443a9d16 "inputselector: Add 2 more calls to check for active pad switch"

Verified against gstinputselector.c/.h as shipped in GStreamer 1.28.6
(the version this recipe builds): applying this patch to the 1.28.6
source and diffing the result against gstinputselector.c/.h at commit
735f21a4c99d60fda474a1b7651dcc33a1215198 (the parent of dd0c2e3e,
i.e. the last commit before any of the above landed) shows no
differences - this patch reproduces that pre-rework file exactly, and
no other, unrelated commit touched this file in between.

